\startchapter[reference=methods, title=Methods]

\startsection[title={Dimensionality Reduction Methods}]

Dimensionality reduction methods embed high dimensional data into a low-dimensional space.
The intrinsic dimensionality of transcriptomic data is low, so 50-100 linear combinations of genes are enough to capture nearly all biologically relevant variation and structure in the data \cite[ding2018].
Global structures can often be faithfully reflected in a 2 or 3 dimensional embedding interpretable by humans.

\startsubsection[title={Spectral decomposition}]

Spectral decomposition is the act of transforming the data into components that each capture a different source of variance in the data.
The components are ranked by their ability to explain the data’s structure.
They can be used as dimensionality reduction methods by discarding components with low enough scores.
This is most useful as a preprocessing step to define a less noisy latent space reflecting the inherent dimensionality of the data, but components can also be directly visualized.

\startsubsubsection[title={Principal Component Analysis}]
Principal component analysis (PCA) is a simple spectral decomposition.
Its components are weighted products of input features (such as transcripts), ranked by the variance each PC explains.
While the intrinsic dimensionality of transcriptomic data is low, more complex datasets cannot be faithfully represented by a low number of (linear) PCs \cite[lenz2016].
Picking a (higher) number of PCs, for example using the jackstraw \cite[chung2015] is a popular a preprocessing step for downstream analysis.

There are a number of related methods that can serve a similar purpose, such as independent component analysis or factor analysis. Furthermore, single cell transcriptomics specific extensions exist
\stopsubsubsection

\startsubsubsection[title={Diffusion Maps}]
Diffusion maps are another spectral decomposition of data.
Their practicality for data analysis was first shown by \inlinecite[coifman2005].
A diffusion map preserves global structures and the pseudotemporal ordering of cells better than t-SNE \cite[haghverdi2015], and the first diffusion components are often sufficient to directly capture and visualize bifurcation events in differentiating cells.

