%https://static-content.springer.com/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fnmeth.3971/MediaObjects/41592_2016_BFnmeth3971_MOESM373_ESM.pdf

A diffusion map consists of the eigenvectors $s_{p·}$ of a matrix of transition probabilities between cells.
The transition probabilities are derived from a diffusion model.
There are several flavors of diffusion models, parametrized over the chosen distance function $\text{dist}$ and the kernel width $σ$ (square root of the gaussian function $Y(c)$’s variance).
\TODO[Clarify how a kernel not based on euclidean distance would be called and defined.]

The most useful set of flavors is the ones independent of local density.
These flavors parametrize their gaussian kernel with a cell-dependent $σ_c$ derived from the local density $σ_c = \text{dist}(c, NN_k(c))$.
\TODO[define dist, NN better.]
Their gaussian function is:

\placeformula
\startformula
Y_c\left(c'\right) =
	∜{2 \over {π σ_c^2}}
	\exp \left(-{
		\left\|\left\|c'-c\right\|\right\|^2
		\over
		σ_c^2
	}\right)
\stopformula

Since gaussian functions are wave functions, the kernel is defined by the interference of two wave functions centered at cells:

\placeformula
\startformula
\startalign
\NC K(c, d) \NC =
	∫_{-∞}^∞ Y_c\left(c'\right) Y_d\left(c'\right) \mathword{d} c'
\NR
\NC \NC =
	√{2 σ_c σ_d \over σ_c^2+σ_d^2}
	\exp \left(-{
		\|\|c-d\|\|^2
		\over
		2\left(σ_c^2+σ_d^2\right)
	}\right)
\NR
\stopalign
\stopformula

With a kernel function defined, the connectivities $W_{cd}$ between cells can be are defined as follows:

\placeformula
\startformula
W_{cd} = {K(c, d) \over Z(c) Z(d)}
\stopformula

\placeformula
\startformula
Z(c) = ∑_{d∈\{1,…,C\}} K(c, d)
\stopformula

More commonly today, $W$ is approximated. One approach is to only define $W_{cd}$ for $d∈\{NN_k(c)\}$ instead of all $d∈\{1,…,C\}$, another is employed by UMAP, which uses fuzzy simplical sets derived from $σ_c$ instead of discrete sets of nearest neighbors.

In any case, a transition probability matrix $T$ can be obtained by row-normalizing $W$:

\placeformula
\startformula
\tilde{Z}(c) = ∑_{d∈\{1,…,C\}} W_{cd}
\stopformula

The traditional variant $T_{cd}^{\mathword{asym}}$, mentioned for completeness, is defined as:

\placeformula
\startformula
T_{cd}^{\mathword{asym}} = \tilde{Z}(c)^{-1} W_{cd}
\stopformula

The symmetrical variant $T_{cd}^{\mathword{sym}}$ has several advantages, including computational performance and the ability to reuse the sparsity structure of $W$: 

\placeformula
\startformula
T_{cd}^{\mathword{sym}} = √{\tilde{Z}(c)}^{-1} W_{cd} √{\tilde{Z}(y)}^{-1}
\stopformula

During the parallel development of destiny \cite[angerer2016] and diffusion pseudo time \cite[haghverdi2016], I helped shape the efficient flavor of diffusion maps most used today \TODO[data]. I pioneered the use of a symmetricized kNN graph represented as a symmetric sparse matrix, using this kNN information as a density estimate for the locally scaled kernel $K$ and its sparsity structure for efficient calculation of the symmetric transition probability matrix $T$.

\stopsubsubsection

\stopsubsection

\startsubsection[title={Learned embeddings}]
% https://nbisweden.github.io/workshop-scRNAseq/slides2019/Dimensionality_reduction_P_Czarnewski_2019.pdf
In order to optimize the embedding for certain use cases, nonlinear embedding functions can be learned.
A common goal is to represent relationships in the data as faithfully as possible in a 2- or 3-dimensional embedding that can be displayed on a screen or paper.

One such machine learning driven method, t-distributed stochastic neighborhood embedding (t-SNE, \inlinecite[vandermaaten2008]), remains the most popular dimensionality reduction method \cite[kobak2019].
The more recent method UMAP \cite[mcinnes2018] was designed to fix \TODO[what] \cite[becht2018].
\inlinecite[kobak2019] showed that t-SNE with a data-driven selection of metaparameters still outperforms UMAP under some circumstances.

Disadvantages of machine learning based methods are their nondeterministic nature and the lack of a resulting function to project new data into an existing embedding.
\stopsubsection

